Where if shines: checking inputs
if is perfect for questions with one answer, like “is the input a number?”. stop() ends the function with a helpful error message:

c_to_f &lt;- function(temp_c, digits = 1) {
  if (!is.numeric(temp_c)) {
    stop("temp_c must be a number, not ", class(temp_c))
  }
  round(temp_c * 9 / 5 + 32, digits)
}

c_to_f("38")

Error in `c_to_f()`:
! temp_c must be a number, not character








Important✏️ TRY IT (5 min): fever status for the measles data




Write a one-value fever_status() with if / else and test it on 38.5.
Write the vectorized version with if_else(), use it in mutate() on measles$temp_c, and count() the result.

✅ Check: a) "fever" b) 1015 “fever” and 148 “no fever”






NoteShow solution






# a)
fever_status_one &lt;- function(temp) {
  if (temp &gt;= 38) {
    "fever"
  } else {
    "no fever"
  }
}

fever_status_one(38.5)

[1] "fever"

# b)
fever_status &lt;- function(temp) {
  if_else(temp &gt;= 38, "fever", "no fever")
}

measles %&gt;%
  mutate(fever_measured = fever_status(temp_c)) %&gt;%
  count(fever_measured)

  fever_measured    n
1          fever 1015
2       no fever  148


38.5 is at least 38, so the one-value version returns "fever". The if_else() version works on all 1163 temperatures at once: 1015 with fever and 148 without.
